<article class="j200-guide">
  <header class="j200-hero">
    <div class="j200-wrap j200-hero-copy">
      <nav class="j200-breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>›</span><a href="/blog/">Blog</a><span>›</span><a href="/category/specific-model-highlights/">Specific Model Highlights</a></nav>
      <p class="j200-eyebrow">The King of the Flat-Tops · 1937 to Today</p>
      <h1>Gibson J-200 Model Guide</h1>
      <p class="j200-dek">The full SJ-200 and J-200 story: model names, construction eras, bridges, bracing, factory moves, related variations, serial numbers, labels, repairs, and the details that separate an original guitar from a convincing parts story.</p>
      <p class="j200-byline">By <a href="/about-me/">Joe Dampt</a> · September 4, 2026</p>
      <div class="j200-actions"><a href="#timeline">See the Timeline</a><a href="/free-appraisal/">Appraise My J-200</a></div>
      <div class="j200-hero-grid">
        <figure><LicensedImage src={hero1948} alt="1948 Gibson SJ-200 sunburst acoustic guitar photographed in its case" width={1800} loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" /><figcaption>1948 SJ-200 · Joe's Archive</figcaption></figure>
        <aside><b>J-200 at a Glance</b><p><strong>Body</strong>About 17 Inches Wide</p><p><strong>Scale</strong>Usually 25 1/2 Inches</p><p><strong>Core Woods</strong>Spruce With Rosewood or Maple</p><p><strong>Signature Parts</strong>Flower Guard and Moustache Bridge</p></aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="j200-intro j200-wrap">
    <p class="j200-drop">A J-200 is easy to recognize from across a room and difficult to authenticate across eighty-nine years. The broad waist, huge lower bout, crown inlays, flowered guard, gold tuners, and moustache bridge form a powerful silhouette. Under that silhouette are several different guitars. A prewar rosewood Super Jumbo, a lightly braced late-1940s maple SJ-200, a double-X 1950s J-200, a Tune-o-matic 1962, a Norlin-era Artist, a Nashville-built 1986, and an early Bozeman example do not share one construction recipe.</p>
    <aside><b>The rule that holds up:</b> date the model from a chain of evidence. The serial narrows the field. The label, woods, bridge, bracing, neck, tuners, finish, and repair history decide whether the rest of the story agrees.</aside>
  </section>

  <nav class="j200-toc j200-wrap" aria-label="On this page"><strong>On This Page</strong><div><a href="#identity">Core Identity</a><a href="#history">History</a><a href="#timeline">Year Changes</a><a href="#bridge">Bridge and Bracing</a><a href="#models">Models and Variations</a><a href="#archive">Photo Archive</a><a href="#dating">Serials and Labels</a><a href="#authenticate">Authentication</a><a href="#repairs">Repairs and Value</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></div></nav>

  <section class="j200-section j200-wrap" id="identity">
    <header class="j200-head"><span>01</span><div><p>The Features That Define the Family</p><h2>Gibson J-200 Core Identity</h2></div></header>
    <p class="j200-lead">The model began as Gibson's biggest and most expensive flat-top. Its price supplied the 200, and Super Jumbo supplied the SJ. Catalogs, labels, and shipping ledgers did not always change names together, which is why SJ-200 and J-200 can both be correct for the same period.</p>
    <div class="j200-table-wrap"><table><caption>The Stable Features and the Moving Targets</caption><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Family Baseline</th><th>What Changes</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Body</td><td>Super Jumbo outline, about 17 inches across the lower bout</td><td>Very early custom dimensions, body depth, and a few later reduced-size models</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Scale</td><td>Usually 25 1/2 inches</td><td>Some earliest custom instruments were longer; measure from nut to twelfth fret and double it</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Top</td><td>Spruce</td><td>Species, thickness, brace pattern, bridge plate, repairs, and finish</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Back and sides</td><td>Rosewood before the postwar change, maple for the classic postwar standard</td><td>Custom orders, J-250R, Custom, Western Classic, Pre-War, and modern rosewood variants</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Neck</td><td>Maple in several laminated forms</td><td>Two-, three-, and five-piece builds, center stringers, volutes, profiles, and occasional variant woods</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fingerboard</td><td>Ebony early, rosewood through much of the postwar standard</td><td>Reissues and special models can return to ebony or use other appointments</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Bridge</td><td>Moustache form on the flagship standard</td><td>Individual saddles, straight saddle, open and closed forms, Tune-o-matic, two- and four-bar inlay, plus non-moustache variants</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Finish</td><td>Sunburst, with natural available after the war</td><td>Ebony, cherry, limited colors, artist finishes, aging packages, and custom work</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <div class="j200-name-note"><b>SJ Does Not Always Mean the Same Guitar</b><p>On this model, SJ means Super Jumbo. Gibson also used SJ for the Southern Jumbo, a smaller slope-shouldered model from a different family. A label that says only SJ should not be read as SJ-200 without body and trim evidence. Use the <a href="/post/identify-vintage-gibson-j45-j50-sj/">vintage Gibson J-45, J-50, and Southern Jumbo identification guide</a> when the body is the smaller slope-shouldered form.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="j200-photo-band" id="history">
    <figure><LicensedImage src={front1950s} alt="Natural 1950s Gibson J-200 with an open moustache bridge and flower pickguard" width={1500} /><figcaption>1950s Natural J-200</figcaption></figure>
    <blockquote>The outline stayed famous while the structure kept moving.<em>Read the era, then the guitar.</em></blockquote>
    <figure><LicensedImage src={front1962} alt="Full front of a 1962 Gibson J-200 in sunburst with a closed moustache bridge" width={1500} /><figcaption>1962 Sunburst J-200</figcaption></figure>
  </section>

  <section class="j200-section j200-wrap">
    <header class="j200-head"><span>02</span><div><p>From Singing Cowboys to Stadium Stages</p><h2>How the Super Jumbo Became the J-200</h2></div></header>
    <div class="j200-columns"><div><h3>Ray Whitley and the First Custom Guitars</h3><p>The starting point is 1937, when Ray Whitley discussed a larger, more theatrical flat-top with Gibson president Guy Hart. Gibson borrowed visual language from the L-5 archtop, including an ornate bound headstock, crown and crest inlays, and a decorated guard. Early customers included Gene Autry, Tex Ritter, Jimmy Wakely, and Ray “Crash” Corrigan. Personalized fingerboards and unusual 12-fret configurations belong to this custom period.</p><p>Those first instruments are dangerous places for rigid checklists. The catalog model was still being worked out, and surviving guitars can preserve features that never became standard. Provenance, ledger entries, factory photographs, and internal construction carry unusual weight on a claimed 1937 or early 1938 example.</p><h3>A $200 Flagship</h3><p>The 1938 catalog called it the Super Jumbo and priced it at $200 before the case. The number soon entered the name as Super Jumbo 200. Gibson also created the lower-priced Super Jumbo 100 with plainer trim and mahogany construction. The 200 remained a custom-order-level purchase for much of its early life, which helps explain the very small prewar shipping totals.</p></div><div><h3>Postwar Maple and a Split Name</h3><p>The postwar guitar changed more than its wood. Gibson advertising used J-200 while labels continued to show SJ-200 into the early 1950s. The normal body wood changed from rosewood to figured maple, the fingerboard and bridge were rosewood, and a natural finish became available. A 1948 such as the sunburst guitar in this archive is the beginning of the familiar postwar identity, but its single-X top is lighter than the double-X construction Gibson restored in 1952.</p><h3>Why Artists Kept Choosing It</h3><p>The guitar's physical scale made it an instant stage object. Its long scale, large air chamber, and maple body can deliver a dry, quick attack beneath a voice, while rosewood versions can add deeper overtones. Elvis Presley, the Everly Brothers, Bob Dylan, Pete Townshend, George Harrison, Tom Petty, Emmylou Harris, and many others used members of the wider Super Jumbo family. Some later received signature editions, but the artist association never replaces the model specification.</p><p>For one documented 1955 example, see the story of <a href="/post/bob-denton-1955-gibson-j200/">Bob Denton's natural Gibson J-200</a>, label number A 20058, and its connection to Eddie Cochran's California rockabilly circle.</p></div></div>
  </section>

  <section class="j200-section j200-dark" id="timeline"><div class="j200-wrap">
    <header class="j200-head"><span>03</span><div><p>The Changes That Move Identification and Sound</p><h2>Gibson J-200 Year-by-Year Timeline</h2></div></header>
    <div class="j200-timeline">{eras.map(([date,title,detail]) => <div><time>{date}</time><section><h3>{title}</h3><p>{detail}</p></section></div>)}</div>
    <p class="j200-warning"><b>Transition dates are ranges.</b> Gibson used parts already on hand, changed catalog copy after production changes, and accepted custom orders. Treat the timeline as an expected feature map, then document every part of the individual guitar.</p>
  </div></section>

  <section class="j200-section j200-wrap" id="bridge">
    <header class="j200-head"><span>04</span><div><p>The Most Useful External and Internal Clues</p><h2>J-200 Bridge and Bracing Changes</h2></div></header>
    <p class="j200-lead">The moustache bridge is the fastest visual clue and one of the least safe parts to date by itself. Bridges crack, lift, rotate, get shaved, and get replaced. Many owners also converted metal adjustable saddles to bone. The bridge footprint, holes, plate, braces, finish, and glue line have to tell one story.</p>
    <div class="j200-bridge-grid">
      <article><figure><LicensedImage src={front1948} alt="1948 Gibson SJ-200 sunburst body with flower pickguard and open moustache bridge" width={1200} /><figcaption>1948 · Open Bridge</figcaption></figure><h3>Early Open Moustache</h3><p>Prewar production begins with an ornate open bridge and six threaded individual bone saddles, then moves toward a conventional straight saddle around 1940 and 1941. Postwar open bridges use the recognizable cutout ends and pearl bars with a fixed saddle. Check the number and shape of pearl pieces, pin line, saddle angle, wood, and footprint.</p></article>
      <article><figure><LicensedImage src={bridge1950s} alt="Close view of a 1950s Gibson J-200 open moustache bridge and natural spruce top" width={1200} /><figcaption>1950s · Fixed Saddle</figcaption></figure><h3>Postwar Fixed Saddle</h3><p>A clean 1950s open bridge should agree with the finish shadow and bridge plate below it. Low saddle exposure can indicate a poor neck angle or a shaved bridge. A taller replacement bridge can hide a neck-angle problem. Measurements matter more than the graceful outline.</p></article>
      <article><figure><LicensedImage src={bridge1962} alt="Close view of the metal Tune-o-matic saddle on a 1962 Gibson J-200 closed moustache bridge" width={1200} /><figcaption>1962 · Tune-o-Matic</figcaption></figure><h3>Closed Bridge and Tune-o-Matic</h3><p>From 1961, the solid bridge gained four pearl bars and an adjustable metal saddle. Beneath it, Gibson used a large plate, bolts, and an added suspended brace with an adjustment screw. Removed braces, filled posts, converted saddles, and replaced plates are common. A conversion can improve a player's response, but it changes originality.</p></article>
      <article><figure><LicensedImage src={bridge1986} alt="Two-bar moustache bridge on an ebony 1986 Gibson J-200" width={1200} /><figcaption>1986 · Two-Bar Form</figcaption></figure><h3>Later Fixed-Saddle Moustache</h3><p>Later J-200 and SJ-200 production returned to fixed saddles and used several two-bar and four-bar moustache interpretations. Modern Standard, Historic, Custom, and artist models can intentionally copy different periods. The bridge style can identify the target specification, but the label and serial establish when Gibson built it.</p></article>
    </div>
    <div class="j200-brace-grid"><div><h3>Early Double-X</h3><p>The first catalog guitars used two wide-angle X structures with ladder braces behind the lower system. This is a distinct prewar design, not the same pattern as the heavier double-X used later.</p></div><div><h3>Light Single-X</h3><p>Introduced during the 1941 and 1942 transition and retained on the first postwar guitars. These late prewar and 1947 to 1951 examples are often prized for a more responsive top.</p></div><div><h3>1952 Double-X</h3><p>Gibson restored double-X bracing with ladder bars and a long plate as body depth increased. The stiffer top separates a 1950s J-200 from its immediate postwar predecessor.</p></div><div><h3>1960s Support Hardware</h3><p>The large bridge plate, bridge bolts, and suspended brace around the Tune-o-matic system add another layer. Missing hardware can be an old tonal modification, not proof that the guitar left Kalamazoo without it.</p></div><div><h3>Norlin Double-X</h3><p>Seventies examples often carry a stout double-X system and large plate. Individual guitars can still sound good, but condition and setup should be judged without pretending the structure matches a late-1940s guitar.</p></div><div><h3>Bozeman Revival</h3><p>Montana production moved back toward traditional X-bracing, and later Historic models use period-targeted scalloping, hide glue, top aging, and other specifications. A historic recipe built recently remains a modern guitar.</p></div></div>
  </section>

  <section class="j200-section j200-dark" id="models"><div class="j200-wrap">
    <header class="j200-head"><span>05</span><div><p>Similar Names, Different Specifications</p><h2>Gibson J-200 Models and Variations</h2></div></header>
    <div class="j200-model-grid">
      <article><b>The Main Line</b><h3>Super Jumbo, SJ-200, and J-200</h3><p>These names trace the flagship family. The name alone does not separate every era. A late-1940s label may say SJ-200 while the catalog says J-200, and recent Gibson production uses SJ-200 again.</p></article>
      <article><b>A Finish Code</b><h3>J-200N</h3><p>The N normally identifies natural finish in postwar records and price lists. It is not automatically a rarer structural model. Compare its year, woods, bracing, bridge, and originality with the sunburst version.</p></article>
      <article><b>The Plainer Relative</b><h3>SJ-100, J-100, and J-100 Xtra</h3><p>The 100 series uses the large-body idea with reduced ornament and period-specific wood choices. It is a real Gibson family, but adding a decorated guard, crown inlays, or a moustache bridge does not turn one into a J-200.</p></article>
      <article><b>The Norlin Branch</b><h3>J-200 Artist</h3><p>Seen from the early 1970s into the 1980s, the Artist can use a slightly narrower Super Jumbo body, Dove-style bridge, double-X bracing, volute, three-piece maple neck, crown inlays, and gold hardware. Read the label rather than assuming every flower-guard jumbo is the standard model.</p></article>
      <article><b>The Rosewood Seventies Model</b><h3>J-250R</h3><p>This higher-priced relative paired the Super Jumbo platform with rosewood and its own appointments. It belongs beside the J-200 history, but the full model stamp and construction keep it from being misrepresented as a prewar-style SJ-200.</p></article>
      <article><b>Reduced-Size Relations</b><h3>J-200 Junior, J-180, and J-185</h3><p>The J-200 Junior and the 16-inch J-180 or J-185 family can carry maple, crown inlays, and related visual language in a smaller body. Measure the lower bout. A smaller outline is a model fact, not a camera illusion.</p></article>
      <article><b>Player-Focused Editions</b><h3>J-200 Studio and EC Models</h3><p>Studio and cutaway acoustic-electric versions can change depth, binding, bridge ornament, neck feel, and onboard electronics. Factory electronics should agree with the model code, routing, jack, controls, and production date.</p></article>
      <article><b>Modern Core Models</b><h3>SJ-200 Standard, Original, and Rosewood</h3><p>Recent Bozeman families include maple standards, period-influenced Original specifications, and rosewood versions. Gibson changes active offerings over time, so a dated factory specification sheet is better evidence than today's product menu.</p></article>
      <article><b>Historic Targets</b><h3>1957 Reissue and Pre-War SJ-200</h3><p>The 1957 interpretation uses maple and a four-bar moustache bridge. Pre-War recreations return to rosewood, ebony, early trim, and period-directed bracing. Reissue serial formats, certificates, labels, and construction distinguish them from originals.</p></article>
      <article><b>High-Trim Builds</b><h3>Custom, Western Classic, and Monarch</h3><p>These can use rosewood, abalone, elaborate bridges, three-piece backs, vine work, precious materials, and one-off details. Exceptional ornament demands stronger factory documentation because skilled independent builders can reproduce the visible decoration.</p></article>
      <article><b>Artist Specifications</b><h3>Signature and Limited Editions</h3><p>Elvis Presley, Bob Dylan, Pete Townshend, Tom Petty, the Everly Brothers, Jimmy Page, Johnny Cash, Brian May, and other editions target particular instruments or stage designs. Some use double guards, black finishes, pickups, cutaways, or twelve strings. The artist name is part of the model identity.</p></article>
      <article><b>A Different Brand</b><h3>Epiphone EJ-200 and Inspired by Gibson</h3><p>Epiphone has built several Super Jumbo versions, including recent Gibson Custom collaborations. They can be excellent instruments and can share the outline, but their headstock, label, country of manufacture, materials, serial system, and market are separate from a Gibson-built J-200.</p></article>
    </div>
    <p class="j200-warning"><b>Custom order does not mean undocumented.</b> Gibson did depart from catalog specification. A credible special order should be supported by period construction, factory paperwork, ledger information, a matching model code, or a chain of provenance. “They must have custom made it” is not evidence.</p>
  </div></section>

  <section class="j200-archive" id="archive"><div class="j200-wrap">
    <header class="j200-head"><span>06</span><div><p>Five Guitars From Joe's Drive Archive</p><h2>Gibson J-200 Photo Timeline</h2></div></header>

    <article class="j200-era"><div><p class="j200-eyebrow">Postwar Restart</p><h3>1948 Gibson SJ-200</h3><p>This sunburst example shows the postwar maple formula before the 1952 bracing revision. Its script logo, crown, white oval label, open bridge, deeply figured two-piece back, multi-ply trim, and flower guard should be read together. The bridge and guard are visible clues; the single-X interior is the era-defining construction.</p></div><div class="j200-gallery j200-gallery-4"><figure><LicensedImage src={headstock1948} alt="Bound headstock of a 1948 Gibson SJ-200 with script logo and crown inlay" width={1000} /><figcaption>Script Logo and Crown</figcaption></figure><figure><LicensedImage src={label1948} alt="White oval Gibson label inside a 1948 SJ-200" width={1000} /><figcaption>White Oval Label</figcaption></figure><figure><LicensedImage src={back1948} alt="Figured maple back of a 1948 Gibson SJ-200 in sunburst" width={1000} /><figcaption>Figured Maple Back</figcaption></figure><figure><LicensedImage src={front1948} alt="Sunburst top of a 1948 Gibson SJ-200" width={1000} /><figcaption>Postwar Sunburst Top</figcaption></figure></div></article>

    <article class="j200-era"><div><p class="j200-eyebrow">Classic Fifties Trim</p><h3>Natural 1950s Gibson J-200</h3><p>The blond top makes the bridge footprint, guard outline, and top condition easy to inspect. The gold tuners, laminated maple neck construction, figured back, crown inlays, and open fixed-saddle bridge are strong period clues. None should be used alone, because tuners, guards, bridges, and even necks are replaceable.</p></div><div class="j200-gallery j200-gallery-5"><figure><LicensedImage src={front1950s} alt="Natural 1950s Gibson J-200 full front" width={1000} /><figcaption>Natural Finish</figcaption></figure><figure><LicensedImage src={headstock1950s} alt="Front of a 1950s Gibson J-200 bound headstock" width={1000} /><figcaption>Bound Headstock</figcaption></figure><figure><LicensedImage src={back1950s} alt="Flame maple back of a natural 1950s Gibson J-200" width={1000} /><figcaption>Flame Maple Back</figcaption></figure><figure><LicensedImage src={heel1950s} alt="Maple neck heel and body joint of a 1950s Gibson J-200" width={1000} /><figcaption>Neck Heel</figcaption></figure><figure><LicensedImage src={tuners1950s} alt="Gold tuners on the back of a 1950s Gibson J-200 headstock" width={1000} /><figcaption>Gold Tuners</figcaption></figure></div></article>

    <article class="j200-era"><div><p class="j200-eyebrow">The 1961 Construction Package</p><h3>1962 Gibson J-200</h3><p>The closed four-bar bridge and Tune-o-matic saddle identify the new external system. The red-brown guard, crown inlays, faint impressed serial, orange oval label, gold tuners, and flame maple back complete the visible record. Inspection inside should look for the large plate, bolts, enlarged neck block, and suspended brace associated with this system, plus evidence of any later removal.</p></div><div class="j200-gallery j200-gallery-5"><figure><LicensedImage src={guard1962} alt="Flower pickguard and closed four-bar bridge on a 1962 Gibson J-200" width={1000} /><figcaption>Guard and Closed Bridge</figcaption></figure><figure><LicensedImage src={headstock1962} alt="Front of the bound headstock on a 1962 Gibson J-200" width={1000} /><figcaption>1962 Headstock</figcaption></figure><figure><LicensedImage src={back1962} alt="Flame maple back of a 1962 Gibson J-200" width={1000} /><figcaption>Flame Maple Back</figcaption></figure><figure><LicensedImage src={serial1962} alt="Faint impressed serial on the back of a 1962 Gibson J-200 headstock" width={1000} /><figcaption>Impressed Serial</figcaption></figure><figure><LicensedImage src={label1962} alt="Orange oval J-200 label inside a 1962 Gibson acoustic guitar" width={1000} /><figcaption>Orange Oval Label</figcaption></figure></div></article>

    <article class="j200-era"><div><p class="j200-eyebrow">After Kalamazoo, Before Bozeman</p><h3>1986 Gibson J-200</h3><p>Serial 81006505 decodes under Gibson's YDDDYRRR system as 1986, day 100, ranking 505. Gibson's official guide assigns 500 through 999 to Nashville production through 1989. The label still uses the old Kalamazoo address. That disagreement is useful factory-transition evidence, not a reason to discard the serial.</p></div><div class="j200-gallery j200-gallery-6"><figure><LicensedImage src={front1986} alt="Ebony 1986 Gibson J-200 full front in its red-lined case" width={1000} /><figcaption>Ebony Full Front</figcaption></figure><figure><LicensedImage src={back1986} alt="Ebony-finished maple back of a 1986 Gibson J-200" width={1000} /><figcaption>Maple Back Under Ebony</figcaption></figure><figure><LicensedImage src={headstock1986} alt="Bound front headstock of a 1986 Gibson J-200 with logo and crown" width={1000} /><figcaption>Logo and Crown</figcaption></figure><figure><LicensedImage src={serial1986} alt="Serial 81006505 and Made in USA stamp on a 1986 Gibson J-200" width={1000} /><figcaption>Serial 81006505</figcaption></figure><figure><LicensedImage src={label1986} alt="Kalamazoo-address J-200 label with serial 81006505 inside a Nashville-built 1986 Gibson" width={1000} /><figcaption>Kalamazoo-Address Label</figcaption></figure><figure><LicensedImage src={heel1986} alt="Neck heel and body binding on an ebony 1986 Gibson J-200" width={1000} /><figcaption>Neck and Body Joint</figcaption></figure></div></article>

    <article class="j200-era"><div><p class="j200-eyebrow">The Montana Restart</p><h3>1990 Gibson J-200</h3><p>Serial 92840001 reads as 1990, day 284, ranking 001. Gibson's serial guide explains that Bozeman acoustic ranking numbers began at 001 each day after the 1989 move. The oval label names Bozeman, Montana, and the pale laminated maple neck, center stringer, figured body, fixed-saddle moustache bridge, and natural finish create a coherent early-Montana specification.</p></div><div class="j200-gallery j200-gallery-5"><figure><LicensedImage src={front1990} alt="Natural 1990 Gibson J-200 full front in its purple-lined case" width={1000} /><figcaption>Early Bozeman J-200</figcaption></figure><figure><LicensedImage src={label1990} alt="Bozeman Montana J-200 label with serial 92840001" width={1000} /><figcaption>Bozeman Label</figcaption></figure><figure><LicensedImage src={serial1990} alt="Serial 92840001 on the maple headstock of a 1990 Gibson J-200" width={1000} /><figcaption>Serial 92840001</figcaption></figure><figure><LicensedImage src={back1990} alt="Highly figured maple back of a natural 1990 Gibson J-200" width={1000} /><figcaption>Figured Maple Back</figcaption></figure><figure><LicensedImage src={side1990} alt="Figured maple side and body depth of a 1990 Gibson J-200" width={1000} /><figcaption>Maple Rim and Depth</figcaption></figure></div></article>
  </div></section>

  <section class="j200-section j200-dark" id="dating"><div class="j200-wrap">
    <header class="j200-head"><span>07</span><div><p>Numbers Need Physical Support</p><h2>How to Date a Gibson J-200</h2></div></header>
    <div class="j200-dating-grid"><div><h3>Factory Order Numbers</h3><p>Prewar and 1950s factory order numbers are commonly inked inside, often on the neck block. Their letter codes and sequence can narrow a batch even when the label is missing or when a serial range overlaps. The <a href="/how-to-read-gibson-serial-numbers/#fon">Gibson Factory Order Number guide and decoder</a> supplies the full code tables. Photograph the full stamp in place. A detached number or a transcription loses information about ink, wood, location, and finish.</p><h3>Postwar Label Numbers</h3><p>White and orange oval labels can carry a serial and handwritten or typed style. Compare the label number with any headstock or factory order number. The site's <a href="/how-to-read-gibson-serial-numbers/#a-prefix">Gibson A-prefix serial number chart</a> covers the postwar sequence used on examples such as Bob Denton's A 20058 guitar. An authentic paper label can survive a refinish, and a false label can be aged, so the paper cannot carry the whole conclusion.</p><h3>1961 Through the 1970s</h3><p>Headstock serials from this period can overlap years, repeat ranges, or appear with inconsistent impressions. Add construction, hardware, pot or pickup evidence on acoustic-electric variants, and the presence or absence of a Made in USA stamp and volute. Start with the <a href="/how-to-read-gibson-serial-numbers/">complete Gibson serial number dating guide</a>, then use the J-200 construction timeline on this page to test its result.</p></div><div><h3>1977 Through 1989</h3><p>The common eight-digit format is YDDDYRRR: the first and fifth digits form the year, the middle three identify the day, and the last three are the ranking or plant code. Until Kalamazoo closed, rankings 001 through 499 normally point there. Rankings 500 through 999 point to Nashville through 1989.</p><h3>Bozeman From 1989</h3><p>Gibson states that Bozeman acoustic ranking numbers begin at 001 each day. Modern special editions and historic reissues can use other serial conventions, including formats designed to resemble a target vintage year. Read the model code and certificate with the serial.</p><h3>Measure What the Number Cannot Tell You</h3><p>Record the lower bout, body depth, scale, nut width, string spacing, bridge dimensions, neck depth, and weight. Those measurements can expose a smaller Junior, a changed neck, a shaved bridge, or a modern reissue that photographs like an older guitar.</p></div></div>
    <div class="j200-serial-cards"><article><b>81006505</b><span>1986 · Day 100 · Nashville Rank 505</span><p>The Kalamazoo-address label and Nashville ranking code document a factory transition.</p></article><article><b>92840001</b><span>1990 · Day 284 · Bozeman Rank 001</span><p>The Bozeman label, low daily rank, and Montana-period construction agree.</p></article></div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="j200-section j200-wrap" id="authenticate">
    <header class="j200-head"><span>08</span><div><p>A Purchase-Level Inspection Sequence</p><h2>How to Authenticate a Gibson J-200</h2></div></header>
    <p class="j200-lead">Begin with full photographs before loosening old parts. Put the guitar into a likely era, then try to disprove that date with independent clues. The strongest identification asks the fewest features to be exceptions. This follows the site's broader <a href="/post/what-a-serial-number-cant-tell-you/">vintage guitar inspection process beyond the serial number</a>.</p>
    <div class="j200-checks">{checks.map(([title,detail],i) => <div><i>{i+1}</i><h3>{title}</h3><p>{detail}</p></div>)}</div>
    <aside class="j200-warning"><b>Do not authenticate a J-200 from the logo.</b> Logos, guards, bridges, labels, tuners, and serials can all be copied or transplanted. Body geometry and internal tool work are harder to fake, and a conclusion becomes credible when the visible and hidden evidence agree.</aside>
  </section>

  <section class="j200-section j200-dark" id="repairs"><div class="j200-wrap">
    <header class="j200-head"><span>09</span><div><p>Originality, Function, and Market Position</p><h2>J-200 Repairs, Condition, and Value</h2></div></header>
    <p class="j200-lead">A repair is not automatically a reason to reject a guitar. The issue is what was done, why it was needed, how much original material remains, whether the work is stable, and whether the description is accurate. Value starts with sold examples of the same era and variant, then moves for originality, structure, sound, finish, provenance, and timing.</p>
    <div class="j200-condition-grid"><article><b>Usually Accepted Maintenance</b><h3>Neck Reset and Refret</h3><p>A clean neck reset and sympathetic refret can return a valuable guitar to proper geometry. Inspect heel fit, finish disturbance, fingerboard extension, fretboard thickness, nut work, and remaining saddle height. The <a href="/post/how-to-tell-if-your-acoustic-guitar-needs-a-neck-reset/">acoustic guitar neck reset inspection guide</a> explains the geometry checks in detail.</p></article><article><b>Common Structural Work</b><h3>Top Cracks and Loose Braces</h3><p>Pickguard cracks, center seams, and loose braces are common on old flat-tops. Well-fitted cleats and clean glue work are different from wide filled cracks, flattened arch, or heavily sanded interior surfaces.</p></article><article><b>High-Impact Alteration</b><h3>Bridge and Plate Replacement</h3><p>The bridge system sits at the center of J-200 tone and identification. An incorrect footprint, oversized plate, filled Tune-o-matic posts, shaved bridge, or moved pin line can affect structure, sound, and collector value at once.</p></article><article><b>Major Originality Change</b><h3>Refinish and Overspray</h3><p>A full refinish usually moves a guitar into a different market tier. Overspray may be more limited, but it still needs mapping. Check serial impressions, binding edges, protected color, decal or label relationship, sanding, and blacklight response.</p></article><article><b>Parts With Frequent Turnover</b><h3>Guard, Tuners, Nut, and Saddle</h3><p>These replacements are common and can make a guitar far more usable. Original holes, footprints, removed parts, and clear disclosure help preserve the historical record. A reproduction part should not be described as factory original.</p></article><article><b>Evidence That Can Add Confidence</b><h3>Case and Provenance</h3><p>A correct case, receipt, owner photograph, repair invoice, factory letter, or artist documentation can add context and value. Provenance supports the guitar's physical evidence. It does not overrule contradictory construction.</p></article></div>
    <div class="j200-rank"><div><b>Highest Collector Demand</b><h3>Prewar Rosewood</h3><p>Extremely small production, early custom history, rosewood construction, and surviving originality put verified prewar examples in their own category.</p></div><div><b>Postwar Favorites</b><h3>1947 to 1951</h3><p>Maple construction and the lighter single-X top create a distinct early postwar group before the 1952 brace change.</p></div><div><b>Classic Visual Era</b><h3>1950s J-200</h3><p>Open bridge, fixed saddle, flower guard, figured maple, and golden-era trim carry strong demand, with exact bracing and originality deciding the order.</p></div><div><b>Specialist Transition</b><h3>1961 Through the 1960s</h3><p>Closed bridges, adjustable saddles, added support hardware, and neck changes make condition and internal completeness especially important.</p></div><div><b>Case-by-Case Vintage</b><h3>1970s and 1980s</h3><p>Artist specifications, Norlin construction, late Kalamazoo work, and Nashville transition guitars have different markets. Strong examples deserve model-specific comparisons.</p></div><div><b>Modern Collecting</b><h3>Early Bozeman and Historic Models</h3><p>Early Montana guitars, rare signatures, Custom Shop work, and faithful reissues can be desirable, but they form modern categories rather than inheriting vintage dates.</p></div></div>
    <div class="j200-cta"><div><p class="j200-eyebrow">Have a J-200 With a Complicated Story?</p><h3>Send the Evidence Before You Price It.</h3><p>Include full front and back views, both sides of the headstock, the label, serial, neck block, bridge, pickguard, heel, tuners, interior bracing, bridge plate, case, and every repair you know about.</p></div><div><a href="/free-appraisal/">Request a Free Appraisal</a><a href="/sell-my-gibson-guitar/">Sell a Vintage Gibson</a></div></div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="j200-production"><div class="j200-wrap"><p class="j200-eyebrow">Factory Shipping Records · 1938 to 1979</p><b>6,913</b><h2>Documented J-200 and SJ-200 Shipments</h2><p>Shipping totals are not exact production counts. Guitars could be returned, reshipped, custom ordered, or recorded inconsistently. The records still show the scale of the model: 96 shipments from 1938 through 1941, 2,044 from 1948 through 1959, 2,376 in the 1960s, and 2,397 in the 1970s. Natural and sunburst totals are combined here.</p><a href="/post/gibson-shipping-totals-1948-1979/">See Gibson Production and Shipping Totals</a></div></section>

  <FAQ faqs={faqs} mainTitle="Gibson J-200" subTitle="Frequently Asked Questions" sectionId="faq" openFirst={true} headingLevel="h2" />

  <section class="j200-closing j200-wrap"><div><p class="j200-eyebrow">The Last Word</p><h2>Authenticate the Entire J-200.</h2><p>A serial suggests a date. A label suggests a model and factory. The bridge and tuners suggest an era. The woods, body, neck, bracing, bridge plate, finish, tool marks, repairs, and provenance decide whether all of those suggestions can belong to the same guitar.</p></div></section>
